John, thanks for joining us.

00:01:36.003 --> 00:01:38.503
- Sal, thanks for having me,
and you know what big fans

00:01:38.503 --> 00:01:41.073
we are in the work you do at Khan Academy,

00:01:41.073 --> 00:01:42.783
at AT&amp;T, so it's a real treat for me

00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:44.533
to be part of the curriculum today.

00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:47.843
- Well, no, and I do have to thank y'all.

00:01:47.843 --> 00:01:50.503
I mean, it is a really big
deal the work that y'all do.

00:01:50.503 --> 00:01:52.743
I mean, on a bunch of
levels that we're gonna talk

00:01:52.743 --> 00:01:55.143
a lot about things like internet
access and digital divide,

00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:57.063
because obviously we can't do our work

00:01:57.063 --> 00:01:59.423
and reach the folks who need
it most without that existing.

00:01:59.423 --> 00:02:01.803
But y'all over the years,
not just during COVID,

00:02:01.803 --> 00:02:03.583
have been big supporters of Khan Academy,

00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:04.953
and that really means a lot.

00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:06.163
So everyone listening,

00:02:06.163 --> 00:02:07.963
if you're able to benefit
from Khan Academy,

00:02:07.963 --> 00:02:10.213
it's AT&amp;T and organizations like it

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:13.833
that really allow us to do this work.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.433
But John, maybe a good place to start.

00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:20.093
You are relatively new in the job at AT&amp;T,

00:02:20.093 --> 00:02:24.543
AT&amp;T is a storied institution
for maybe the younger people

00:02:24.543 --> 00:02:25.763
who didn't know, it's not a startup,

00:02:25.763 --> 00:02:28.116
it's a very well established.

00:02:30.123 --> 00:02:31.933
What was kind of your thought process

00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:35.816
as you've taken the role,
and as you're taking this,

00:02:35.816 --> 00:02:38.253
I would call AT&amp;T is kind
of one of the institutions

00:02:38.253 --> 00:02:40.283
of America or the world.

00:02:40.283 --> 00:02:42.233
How are you viewing that?

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:45.203
- Well it's interesting you
use the analogy startup,

00:02:45.203 --> 00:02:48.993
and I know you kind of did it
in jest, but the reality is,

00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:51.653
well, it's a storied
institution that's been around

00:02:51.653 --> 00:02:54.863
for a very, very long time.

00:02:54.863 --> 00:02:57.693
Just about every line of
business that we operate in

00:02:57.693 --> 00:02:59.643
right now is in a major state

00:02:59.643 --> 00:03:02.623
of transition and transformation.

00:03:02.623 --> 00:03:06.023
And so even an established
company like AT&amp;T,

00:03:06.023 --> 00:03:08.703
I think there's so many
different industries

00:03:08.703 --> 00:03:10.933
and so many large established businesses

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:13.893
because of not only COVID,
but what's just going on

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.893
in the economy in general,
that are all undergoing

00:03:16.893 --> 00:03:19.123
some pretty rapid and significant change,

00:03:19.123 --> 00:03:21.393
and we're no exception to that.

00:03:21.393 --> 00:03:24.363
And as a result of that,
while it's a big company,

00:03:24.363 --> 00:03:27.003
and we have several large
lines of businesses,

00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:29.713
each one of those inside
the company is going through

00:03:29.713 --> 00:03:31.663
its own transition and transformation.

00:03:31.663 --> 00:03:35.643
And so it's a very exciting
time to come into this role.

00:03:35.643 --> 00:03:38.533
It's a bit of a humbling
time as you indicated,

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:41.503
which so many of the
things that we do right now

00:03:41.503 --> 00:03:44.503
in this COVID environment
are dependent on the networks

00:03:44.503 --> 00:03:47.067
and the infrastructure that we provide.

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:49.103
And I reminded that of the bad everyday,

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:52.763
sometimes positive comments
coming back from our customers,

00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:56.123
talking about how they're
surviving and living their lives

00:03:56.123 --> 00:03:57.703
and doing the things that they must do

00:03:57.703 --> 00:04:00.193
because of the services we provide.

00:04:00.193 --> 00:04:02.933
But also, I sometimes
hear about that moment

00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:05.923
when the service doesn't
work on a given day,

00:04:05.923 --> 00:04:08.493
and just how significant
an interruption is

00:04:08.493 --> 00:04:11.583
to somebody's wellbeing
and their daily existence

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.793
if that happens, and
puts a lot of pressure

00:04:13.793 --> 00:04:14.703
on the organization.

00:04:14.703 --> 00:04:17.793
But I've been really
pleased with how overall

00:04:17.793 --> 00:04:21.503
the businesses perform, how
our employees stepped up

00:04:21.503 --> 00:04:24.863
to ensuring services work
during this critical time,

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:28.503
and I couldn't be more happy
about the new relevance

00:04:28.503 --> 00:04:30.743
that I think people have
found in our services

00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:31.626
in this moment.

00:04:33.503 --> 00:04:35.173
- And where do you see your focal point?

00:04:35.173 --> 00:04:37.173
I mean, given what's going on in COVID,

00:04:37.173 --> 00:04:40.223
you've written a lot about the need for

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:41.953
solving the digital divide issue.

00:04:41.953 --> 00:04:44.753
Obviously y'all have
been key actors in this.

00:04:44.753 --> 00:04:47.133
Y'all have really been doing
great things when it comes to

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:49.183
getting communities
through this COVID period,

00:04:49.183 --> 00:04:51.973
especially communities that
did not have internet access,

00:04:51.973 --> 00:04:53.533
making it far more accessible.

00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:55.373
What are the programs that
you've done in the short term?

00:04:55.373 --> 00:04:57.003
And how are you thinking
about this long term?

00:04:57.003 --> 00:04:59.686
How do we solve the digital divide issue?

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:04.643
- You know, it's interesting
because in some regards,

00:05:04.643 --> 00:05:07.123
there's that old analogy of the old house

00:05:07.123 --> 00:05:08.673
that needs a lot of structural work,

00:05:08.673 --> 00:05:12.003
that sometimes you put a little
bit of plaster or spackling

00:05:12.003 --> 00:05:14.343
over cracks to hide them.

00:05:14.343 --> 00:05:17.073
And I would say maybe pre-COVID,

00:05:17.073 --> 00:05:19.383
that might've been the
state of internet access

00:05:19.383 --> 00:05:21.643
in the United States in some regards.

00:05:21.643 --> 00:05:24.513
Some very robust and
capable wireless networks

00:05:24.513 --> 00:05:25.346
have been built.

00:05:25.346 --> 00:05:28.653
And many individuals
were getting their access

00:05:28.653 --> 00:05:31.483
to the internet and
frankly, living their life

00:05:31.483 --> 00:05:34.893
in a pretty okay fashion with wireless

00:05:34.893 --> 00:05:37.003
as their answer to the problem.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:41.553
And they made a basic utility
and value call and said

00:05:41.553 --> 00:05:43.103
for what I need to do in my life,

00:05:43.103 --> 00:05:45.123
and the things I need access for,

00:05:45.123 --> 00:05:47.523
having a smartphone is adequate,

00:05:47.523 --> 00:05:49.763
and that's given that
I've gotta worry about

00:05:49.763 --> 00:05:51.003
putting food on the table

00:05:51.003 --> 00:05:53.813
or going to see the doctor next month.

00:05:53.813 --> 00:05:56.213
It was a luxury to think about having both

00:05:56.213 --> 00:05:59.083
a wireless connection
and a fixed connection,

00:05:59.083 --> 00:06:02.283
so many people chose not to do that.

00:06:02.283 --> 00:06:05.743
And as a result of COVID,
I think what we've seen is,

00:06:05.743 --> 00:06:07.913
well that might've worked before,

00:06:07.913 --> 00:06:11.263
somebody's at home and is
having to live on the internet

00:06:11.263 --> 00:06:15.903
24 hours a day, sit in a
classroom, watch teacher,

00:06:15.903 --> 00:06:18.913
keep their attention on
maybe a multi-screen format

00:06:18.913 --> 00:06:22.563
with a speaker and other
content showing up.

00:06:22.563 --> 00:06:24.483
There's limitations to
that three and a half

00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:27.373
or four inch screen that
everybody depends on

00:06:27.373 --> 00:06:29.523
and carries around in
their pocket everyday.

00:06:29.523 --> 00:06:31.623
And there's limitations in the robustness

00:06:31.623 --> 00:06:34.493
of a wireless network
compared to fixed networks.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:37.933
So we've really seen that this dynamic of

00:06:37.933 --> 00:06:41.263
the 17 million students
that don't have access

00:06:41.263 --> 00:06:45.493
to fixed broadband becomes a
problem in this environment.

00:06:45.493 --> 00:06:47.415
And when you think about all the work that

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:52.413
companies, organizations, sorry,
like yourselves have done,

00:06:52.413 --> 00:06:55.673
in building great curriculum
that can be consumed at home,

00:06:55.673 --> 00:06:57.743
you really need that
robust infrastructure.

00:06:57.743 --> 00:07:00.773
You need the ability to
sit with a larger screen

00:07:00.773 --> 00:07:01.853
and have an experience.

00:07:01.853 --> 00:07:04.793
And so, we're looking at this twofold.

00:07:04.793 --> 00:07:07.353
One is, there's a public policy issue

00:07:07.353 --> 00:07:08.533
that needs to be dealt with,

00:07:08.533 --> 00:07:10.883
and we're leaning into that at AT&amp;T.

00:07:10.883 --> 00:07:13.803
In fact, I'm starting
to orient the company

00:07:13.803 --> 00:07:15.253
and all of the work we're doing

00:07:15.253 --> 00:07:17.203
around our charitable giving,

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:20.293
what we're trying to
do in policy advocacy,

00:07:20.293 --> 00:07:22.003
how we're engaging our employees

00:07:22.003 --> 00:07:23.823
and their working communities,

00:07:23.823 --> 00:07:26.573
to start working on the issues that effect

00:07:26.573 --> 00:07:28.873
those 17 million students,

00:07:28.873 --> 00:07:32.743
and probably 24 million
households that really don't have

00:07:32.743 --> 00:07:35.503
that robust fixed access to the internet.

00:07:35.503 --> 00:07:38.713
And that's policy shifts
that need to occur.

00:07:38.713 --> 00:07:41.763
In some cases, we've got a lot of subsidy

00:07:41.763 --> 00:07:44.653
that are part of our tax structure,

00:07:44.653 --> 00:07:48.183
that maybe is not being directed
as effectively as it could.

00:07:48.183 --> 00:07:52.533
In some cases, maybe we need
new ways to subsidize service

00:07:52.533 --> 00:07:54.923
for those who can't afford to pay for it,

00:07:54.923 --> 00:07:58.583
and trying to get that
policy structure proper.

00:07:58.583 --> 00:08:00.323
I would also tell you,
there's certain things

00:08:00.323 --> 00:08:03.353
that we need to do to
facilitate the ecosystem.

00:08:03.353 --> 00:08:07.073
We know that just providing
discounted access isn't enough.

00:08:07.073 --> 00:08:11.853
We provide $10 a month access
for low-income households,

00:08:11.853 --> 00:08:14.693
is one of the services we provide at AT&amp;T.

00:08:14.693 --> 00:08:17.363
We found that wasn't enough
because many homes don't have

00:08:17.363 --> 00:08:20.843
access to the tablet or the
laptop computer that they need

00:08:20.843 --> 00:08:23.593
to really use that access
in an effective way.

00:08:23.593 --> 00:08:27.463
So putting vehicles in place
where we started to donate

00:08:27.463 --> 00:08:30.743
money into entities that helped solve

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:34.013
that complete ecosystem
of what a household needs

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:36.983
for distance learning and
access to the internet.

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:41.033
And then we want this to be
done in a sustainable fashion.

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:44.493
And we think that you need
good robust infrastructure.

00:08:44.493 --> 00:08:47.633
And while we're continuing
to invest aggressively

00:08:47.633 --> 00:08:49.723
in the core parts of our business,

00:08:49.723 --> 00:08:51.093
the core parts of our network,

00:08:51.093 --> 00:08:54.103
and we'll be making some
announcements here shortly

00:08:54.103 --> 00:08:56.833
about stepping up fiber investment.

00:08:56.833 --> 00:08:58.843
Look, there's parts of the United States

00:08:58.843 --> 00:09:00.723
that just aren't that dense.

00:09:00.723 --> 00:09:02.593
And as a result of that,
we need to think about

00:09:02.593 --> 00:09:06.353
policy that supports
access into rural areas

00:09:06.353 --> 00:09:10.133
with adequate broadband to be
able to do most of the things

00:09:10.133 --> 00:09:11.870
that many households take for granted

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:16.443
in suburban, urban areas
and starting to try to drive

00:09:16.443 --> 00:09:18.326
policy adoption in that regard.

00:09:19.623 --> 00:09:21.573
- I'm curious, I actually
I wanna double click on

00:09:21.573 --> 00:09:23.313
each of those, but on that last point,

00:09:23.313 --> 00:09:25.403
just kind of the telecom nerd inside of me

00:09:25.403 --> 00:09:27.473
wants to make sure I understand thing.

00:09:27.473 --> 00:09:31.283
For everyone listening, you
can dig up kind of the ground

00:09:31.283 --> 00:09:34.833
and put fiber in it, which is
a very expensive proposition.

00:09:34.833 --> 00:09:37.643
And then that can get
really good quality access

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:38.476
to a lot of folks.

00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:41.303
But to your point, if
you're in some rural place,

00:09:41.303 --> 00:09:45.143
it could be very cost prohibitive
to make sure that every,

00:09:45.143 --> 00:09:49.023
let's call it farm, has
fiber running through it.

00:09:49.023 --> 00:09:51.873
But you think that there's
ways to do wireless access

00:09:51.873 --> 00:09:54.053
that could get us close
or have the sufficiently

00:09:54.053 --> 00:09:59.033
high bandwidth and reliability
to meet those folks' needs.

00:09:59.033 --> 00:10:02.703
- If you think about it from
a public policy perspective,

00:10:02.703 --> 00:10:06.413
you go back decades in the
United States had a policy

00:10:06.413 --> 00:10:09.993
that it felt like
everybody should get access

00:10:09.993 --> 00:10:11.143
to the telephone network.

00:10:11.143 --> 00:10:14.703
And that universal access
construct was established

00:10:14.703 --> 00:10:18.553
many years ago with overt
policy around subsidy,

00:10:18.553 --> 00:10:22.073
around how do we make sure
that even the farmhouse

00:10:22.073 --> 00:10:25.263
in a rural area was able
to get on the network.

00:10:25.263 --> 00:10:28.393
But if everybody paid their
true cost of that service,

00:10:28.393 --> 00:10:29.593
there's no way that farmhouse

00:10:29.593 --> 00:10:30.763
ever would have been connected.

00:10:30.763 --> 00:10:33.523
The cost would have been so prohibitive,

00:10:33.523 --> 00:10:35.595
nobody would've ever built those

00:10:35.595 --> 00:10:38.923
small twisted copper
pair wires to that home.

00:10:38.923 --> 00:10:42.693
So we average pricing
across the United States,

00:10:42.693 --> 00:10:45.993
with the deliberate
policy approach to ensure

00:10:45.993 --> 00:10:49.503
we eventually got 98% of US households

00:10:49.503 --> 00:10:50.983
onto the telephone network.

00:10:50.983 --> 00:10:53.423
We're at that same
moment for the internet.

00:10:53.423 --> 00:10:56.413
And when we think about
solving that problem,

00:10:56.413 --> 00:10:58.973
if that's the case, there's
probably going to be

00:10:58.973 --> 00:11:01.563
some degree of subsidy that comes in,

00:11:01.563 --> 00:11:04.523
which may mean public
money, taxpayer money.

00:11:04.523 --> 00:11:07.093
And I don't think the answer
to that is that we wanna go

00:11:07.093 --> 00:11:11.083
and overbuild and have
fiber out to a farmhouse,

00:11:11.083 --> 00:11:14.113
where it might cost 10
times the amount it does

00:11:14.113 --> 00:11:16.493
in a suburban or rural area.

00:11:16.493 --> 00:11:19.193
But can we get the benefits
of getting on the internet

00:11:19.193 --> 00:11:22.883
with adequate bandwidth and
maybe a lower cost alternative,

00:11:22.883 --> 00:11:26.453
maybe something that's
only two times as expensive

00:11:26.453 --> 00:11:29.633
is what it might take in
a urban or suburban area,

00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:33.013
but still get most of the
benefits of being on the internet?

00:11:33.013 --> 00:11:36.843
And so one of the policy
positions we believe is important

00:11:36.843 --> 00:11:40.253
is a technology agnostic point of view,

00:11:40.253 --> 00:11:43.773
get the most effective way
to get the right amount

00:11:43.773 --> 00:11:45.353
of bandwidth out to every home.

00:11:45.353 --> 00:11:48.333
And in some cases, to your
point, it might be using

00:11:48.333 --> 00:11:53.046
the latest technology around
wireless services like 5G.

00:11:54.333 --> 00:11:57.463
But in some cases, the answer
may be a different technology,

00:11:57.463 --> 00:12:00.633
possibly low Earth orbiting satellites,

00:12:00.633 --> 00:12:03.673
and what's coming up
with Leo capabilities,

00:12:03.673 --> 00:12:05.803
and that might be the more effective way

00:12:05.803 --> 00:12:09.373
to get to some highly rural households.

00:12:09.373 --> 00:12:12.163
And in some areas, there
may be a opportunity

00:12:12.163 --> 00:12:15.913
is infrastructure's being
deployed for power, electricity

00:12:15.913 --> 00:12:18.183
to also piggyback fiber in there.

00:12:18.183 --> 00:12:20.533
The point is, you wanna
do the right thing,

00:12:20.533 --> 00:12:22.763
at the right time, and the right house,

00:12:22.763 --> 00:12:27.113
but not overspend taxpayer
money to gold plate things

00:12:27.113 --> 00:12:30.013
that ultimately don't
need to be gold plated.

00:12:30.013 --> 00:12:32.343
And we're trying to
provide some frameworks now

00:12:32.343 --> 00:12:36.243
as we work with policymakers
on understanding where those

00:12:36.243 --> 00:12:38.573
cutoffs are and how we
think about that deployment

00:12:38.573 --> 00:12:40.776
of infrastructure moving forward.

00:12:42.003 --> 00:12:45.223
- And quick technical question,
what's a Leo capability?

00:12:45.223 --> 00:12:47.923
- So if you think about
satellite technology

00:12:47.923 --> 00:12:51.233
that's been in place for many decades,

00:12:51.233 --> 00:12:54.673
typically you put a satellite
way up high in orbit,

00:12:54.673 --> 00:12:57.443
and it sits over a particular
part of the United States

00:12:57.443 --> 00:13:00.363
and it's geostationary, and it follows

00:13:00.363 --> 00:13:03.523
that particular part of
the geography constantly.

00:13:03.523 --> 00:13:06.993
With Leo, these satellites
are much smaller.

00:13:06.993 --> 00:13:08.863
- Oh, lower, I see.

00:13:08.863 --> 00:13:10.763
- Much less expensive.

00:13:10.763 --> 00:13:15.163
You put them up much
lower in the atmosphere.

00:13:15.163 --> 00:13:16.553
They don't last as long,

00:13:16.553 --> 00:13:19.003
but because they're so
much less expensive,

00:13:19.003 --> 00:13:23.183
you can cover a much broader
part of the United States

00:13:23.183 --> 00:13:27.223
with allowing them to work in
a non-geostationary fashion

00:13:27.223 --> 00:13:28.803
and circle the globe.

00:13:28.803 --> 00:13:31.783
And this new technology
opens up some opportunities

00:13:31.783 --> 00:13:34.536
in some rural areas, that
weren't there before.

00:13:35.732 --> 00:13:38.563
- And what you just described
is do it essentially saying,

00:13:38.563 --> 00:13:39.403
do it fractional.

00:13:39.403 --> 00:13:41.073
There's multiple ways of
getting internet access.

00:13:41.073 --> 00:13:43.843
It could be fiber, it could be low Earth

00:13:43.843 --> 00:13:47.413
orbiting satellites, it
could be 5G networks.

00:13:47.413 --> 00:13:48.993
Is there a resistance of this?

00:13:48.993 --> 00:13:51.123
Or is it just kind of we
have old laws on the books

00:13:51.123 --> 00:13:54.426
that tend to be very focused
on landlines essentially?

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.683
- I think anytime you
get into a policy debate,

00:13:58.683 --> 00:14:01.953
sometimes there are those
that are very knowledgeable

00:14:01.953 --> 00:14:04.493
about a policy and very well-informed.

00:14:04.493 --> 00:14:06.443
There are others who maybe
tend to gravitate more

00:14:06.443 --> 00:14:07.303
to the soundbite.

00:14:07.303 --> 00:14:10.123
And the soundbite is
everybody needs fiber.

00:14:10.123 --> 00:14:13.273
And certainly in an ideal
world, would it be great

00:14:13.273 --> 00:14:15.873
if every household in the United States

00:14:15.873 --> 00:14:16.843
is connected to fiber?

00:14:16.843 --> 00:14:18.443
Sure, that'd be wonderful.

00:14:18.443 --> 00:14:19.833
But again, I don't think that's

00:14:19.833 --> 00:14:22.536
the optimal use of public funds.

00:14:23.383 --> 00:14:25.453
One of the things that people
like to go and reference

00:14:25.453 --> 00:14:27.082
is they say, well look Korea,

00:14:27.082 --> 00:14:31.383
how can so many homes in
Korea be connected to fiber?

00:14:31.383 --> 00:14:35.013
Well, Korea actually isn't quite the size

00:14:35.013 --> 00:14:36.503
of the United States.

00:14:36.503 --> 00:14:39.363
They don't have square states
sitting in the middle of it

00:14:39.363 --> 00:14:42.663
with a lot of open
territory and pasture land,

00:14:42.663 --> 00:14:44.593
like the United States has.

00:14:44.593 --> 00:14:47.423
So when you have a vertical society,

00:14:47.423 --> 00:14:49.603
the ability to get fiber
into most households

00:14:49.603 --> 00:14:51.323
is quite a bit different
than when you have

00:14:51.323 --> 00:14:55.873
a more horizontal society
like the United States,

00:14:55.873 --> 00:14:58.103
and you have to use
different solutions to that.

00:14:58.103 --> 00:15:01.753
So I think those that are
informed and know the engineering

00:15:01.753 --> 00:15:03.242
and the economics behind it,

00:15:03.242 --> 00:15:07.453
understand that this needs
to be a technology agnostic

00:15:07.453 --> 00:15:09.973
solution, that we need
to use all the tools

00:15:09.973 --> 00:15:13.113
that we have in the toolbox
to address this effectively,

00:15:13.113 --> 00:15:17.443
and frankly fast because
entrenching fiber everywhere

00:15:17.443 --> 00:15:20.303
takes time, whereas
some wireless solutions

00:15:20.303 --> 00:15:24.383
may get a lot of bandwidth
out fairly quickly because of

00:15:24.383 --> 00:15:26.535
the nature of how that
infrastructure is deployed.

00:15:26.535 --> 00:15:29.633
But I think it's just informing
folks with the fact-based,

00:15:29.633 --> 00:15:31.413
so that everybody can have a

00:15:31.413 --> 00:15:33.726
fully informed conversation about it.

00:15:34.653 --> 00:15:35.856
- And one thing you've alluded to,

00:15:35.856 --> 00:15:37.933
this kind of notion of
public private partnership.

00:15:37.933 --> 00:15:39.245
And I think Telecom, growing up,

00:15:39.245 --> 00:15:42.010
Telecom was one thing that
I always got confused about.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.853
I was like, okay who's
digging those trenches?

00:15:44.853 --> 00:15:48.283
Is that the government or is
that the AT&amp;T's of the world?

00:15:48.283 --> 00:15:49.833
Who is providing the subsidy?

00:15:49.833 --> 00:15:51.173
Explain how that works.

00:15:51.173 --> 00:15:54.793
My understanding it is, it's
private companies like AT&amp;T,

00:15:54.793 --> 00:15:57.413
that are putting the cost
behind the investment

00:15:57.413 --> 00:16:01.463
by putting the infrastructure,
but the government provides

00:16:01.463 --> 00:16:03.933
subsidies, discounts, incentives.

00:16:03.933 --> 00:16:05.886
How does that interplay work?

00:16:06.813 --> 00:16:08.343
- It's a complicated question

00:16:08.343 --> 00:16:09.543
and there's a lot of layers to it.

00:16:09.543 --> 00:16:11.523
I would tell you, if you think about it

00:16:11.523 --> 00:16:13.413
at its most basic level,

00:16:13.413 --> 00:16:16.843
the policy up to this point
in time in the United States,

00:16:16.843 --> 00:16:19.473
which has largely been
a market based policy

00:16:19.473 --> 00:16:21.083
for broadband infrastructure deployment

00:16:21.083 --> 00:16:23.333
has actually worked quite well.

00:16:23.333 --> 00:16:24.793
If you think about what's happened,

00:16:24.793 --> 00:16:26.173
in coming into the pandemic,

00:16:26.173 --> 00:16:29.033
with these dramatic increases in traffic,

00:16:29.033 --> 00:16:32.243
people are not sitting around
talking about the fact that

00:16:32.243 --> 00:16:34.673
you and I can conduct this session.

00:16:34.673 --> 00:16:37.713
And at the same time, I'm still
not able to watch my Netflix

00:16:37.713 --> 00:16:41.123
down the hall, that most of
that's working pretty well.

00:16:41.123 --> 00:16:44.783
If you live in a urban or suburban setting

00:16:44.783 --> 00:16:45.673
in the United States.

00:16:45.673 --> 00:16:48.013
And so, policy has been really good

00:16:48.013 --> 00:16:50.313
where markets are competitive,

00:16:50.313 --> 00:16:52.683
driving that level of investment.

00:16:52.683 --> 00:16:55.173
But there are places in
the United States where,

00:16:55.173 --> 00:16:59.193
because of the density,
characteristics of how people live,

00:16:59.193 --> 00:17:01.793
that market approach isn't quite enough

00:17:01.793 --> 00:17:03.853
to incent that investment in broadband.

00:17:03.853 --> 00:17:07.233
And there have been
subsidy models coming in

00:17:07.233 --> 00:17:11.523
to try to incent companies
to go get some help

00:17:11.523 --> 00:17:14.193
from different government funds to offset

00:17:14.193 --> 00:17:17.073
the additional costs,
and then go in and build

00:17:17.073 --> 00:17:18.062
in these territories.

00:17:18.062 --> 00:17:21.363
And they've been successful to a degree,

00:17:21.363 --> 00:17:24.333
but they haven't been quite as successful

00:17:24.333 --> 00:17:25.166
as they need to be.

00:17:25.166 --> 00:17:29.373
If there's still a large
number of rural households,

00:17:29.373 --> 00:17:33.083
maybe 24 million non-broadband connected,

00:17:33.083 --> 00:17:36.953
I bet 70, 80% of those are in rural areas

00:17:36.953 --> 00:17:38.353
of the United States.

00:17:38.353 --> 00:17:39.883
We clearly need to think about

00:17:39.883 --> 00:17:42.663
a little bit different subsidy structure.

00:17:42.663 --> 00:17:46.133
And I think this is where as
the government starts to deal

00:17:46.133 --> 00:17:48.024
with things like
infrastructure deployment,

00:17:48.024 --> 00:17:51.793
and maybe the early stages
of a Biden administration,

00:17:51.793 --> 00:17:54.363
getting those incentives
right and explicit,

00:17:54.363 --> 00:17:59.363
and setting companies like
AT&amp;T or cable companies

00:17:59.393 --> 00:18:03.493
or other telcos, to go in and
build that infrastructure.

00:18:03.493 --> 00:18:05.753
That opportunity is in front of us.

00:18:05.753 --> 00:18:08.493
We've had great success
with some of those programs

00:18:08.493 --> 00:18:10.083
in the past.

00:18:10.083 --> 00:18:11.743
Another subsidy program

00:18:11.743 --> 00:18:14.253
that you may be familiar with is E-Rate.

00:18:14.253 --> 00:18:17.543
E-Rate was targeted at
getting all of our schools

00:18:17.543 --> 00:18:19.553
in the United States connected.

00:18:19.553 --> 00:18:23.003
It was a deliberate
subsidy program that cause

00:18:23.003 --> 00:18:28.003
for low cost infrastructure to
be deployed into every school

00:18:28.883 --> 00:18:33.363
and library in the US, it's
been tremendously successful.

00:18:33.363 --> 00:18:35.663
And everybody thought,
well check that box,

00:18:35.663 --> 00:18:38.433
it's great, we've solved
the problem in education.

00:18:38.433 --> 00:18:41.733
Until all of our students
started leaving the school house

00:18:41.733 --> 00:18:43.263
and going home to learn.

00:18:43.263 --> 00:18:44.733
Now the school was connected,

00:18:44.733 --> 00:18:48.133
but we couldn't get to the end
points, the students at home.

00:18:48.133 --> 00:18:51.306
And this is the next stage that
we kinda have to deal with.

00:18:52.583 --> 00:18:55.623
- And given there's likely
to be multiple rounds

00:18:55.623 --> 00:18:58.903
of stimulus going forward
over the next several months.

00:18:58.903 --> 00:19:01.563
If what are the two or three policy levers

00:19:01.563 --> 00:19:06.483
that you hope happens
over the next few months?

00:19:06.483 --> 00:19:09.413
- I think we have to
think about the subsidy

00:19:09.413 --> 00:19:12.633
and look at how it's
used today, and have it

00:19:12.633 --> 00:19:14.953
kind of come together in
a more focused approach.

00:19:14.953 --> 00:19:19.953
So there's $10 billion
over here in rural subsidy,

00:19:20.363 --> 00:19:22.633
there's two and a half billion over here

00:19:22.633 --> 00:19:26.623
in what we do for the next
version of E-Rate in schools,

00:19:26.623 --> 00:19:28.943
there's what we do in
universal service structure

00:19:28.943 --> 00:19:29.776
that comes in.

00:19:29.776 --> 00:19:32.003
I think in some regards,
stepping back and saying,

00:19:32.003 --> 00:19:34.443
if our goal is to get
everybody on the internet,

00:19:34.443 --> 00:19:37.393
let's make sure we take
some of those programs,

00:19:37.393 --> 00:19:40.073
aggregate them, and focus
them, that'd be one.

00:19:40.073 --> 00:19:43.293
Two, look, if you're
going to have to go in

00:19:43.293 --> 00:19:46.183
and do overt subsidy,
then we should think about

00:19:46.183 --> 00:19:48.193
what the right mechanism is to do that.

00:19:48.193 --> 00:19:51.743
And I think in an appropriations fashion,

00:19:51.743 --> 00:19:53.753
Congress ought to be realistic about it.

00:19:53.753 --> 00:19:56.393
Whereas today, some of these subsidies

00:19:56.393 --> 00:19:58.423
are set up on dying infrastructure.

00:19:58.423 --> 00:20:00.793
For example, the Universal Service Fund

00:20:00.793 --> 00:20:03.803
that supports voice
service for access today,

00:20:03.803 --> 00:20:07.733
is built on top of fixed
telephone line service.

00:20:07.733 --> 00:20:11.093
Well, if fixed telephone
line services are doing this,

00:20:11.093 --> 00:20:13.413
and there's fewer and fewer subscribers,

00:20:13.413 --> 00:20:15.293
what happens to that subsidy pool?

00:20:15.293 --> 00:20:17.103
It disappears eventually,

00:20:17.103 --> 00:20:20.493
as everybody goes to
wireless services for voice.

00:20:20.493 --> 00:20:22.523
So we need to step back,

00:20:22.523 --> 00:20:24.413
make sure that those subsidy structures

00:20:24.413 --> 00:20:27.723
are put on mechanisms
that are sustainable,

00:20:27.723 --> 00:20:30.223
and that are fair to
the industry in total.

00:20:30.223 --> 00:20:32.893
I don't think we should be
limiting our thought process

00:20:32.893 --> 00:20:36.563
around how we attach that tax structure

00:20:36.563 --> 00:20:39.023
to a particular product or service.

00:20:39.023 --> 00:20:42.813
The internet has changed so
much how people make their money

00:20:42.813 --> 00:20:44.773
and what services they use.

00:20:44.773 --> 00:20:47.799
And we think about how the
edge providers, the Facebooks,

00:20:47.799 --> 00:20:50.693
the Googles, the Amazons of the world,

00:20:50.693 --> 00:20:54.283
who have benefited so much
from infrastructure deployment

00:20:54.283 --> 00:20:56.453
and the capabilities of the internet,

00:20:56.453 --> 00:20:59.183
maybe we need to think
about the base under which

00:20:59.183 --> 00:21:03.056
we subsidize to come back
on a broader set of services

00:21:03.056 --> 00:21:05.863
as we go forward, that are more relevant

00:21:05.863 --> 00:21:07.923
for the going forward position,

00:21:07.923 --> 00:21:10.663
and not based on the decades of old,

00:21:10.663 --> 00:21:13.023
when we think about
voice telephone service.

00:21:13.023 --> 00:21:14.593
So I think that's a dynamic

00:21:14.593 --> 00:21:16.843
that we probably need
to come to grips with.

00:21:16.843 --> 00:21:19.883
And then look, understanding
exactly where the problems are.

00:21:19.883 --> 00:21:23.072
Believe it or not, a country
is as effective and capable

00:21:23.072 --> 00:21:27.903
as United States, we actually
don't have really good data

00:21:27.903 --> 00:21:30.052
and information around
where our problems are,

00:21:30.052 --> 00:21:33.211
on how infrastructure's deployed.

00:21:33.211 --> 00:21:36.723
AT&amp;T has a point of view on
the markets that we serve,

00:21:36.723 --> 00:21:38.443
in the homes that we handle,

00:21:38.443 --> 00:21:40.543
but nobody really has a good picture of

00:21:40.543 --> 00:21:43.293
what the United States
looks like in total,

00:21:43.293 --> 00:21:46.253
because there are places
in the US we don't service.

00:21:46.253 --> 00:21:48.193
We don't have infrastructure.

00:21:48.193 --> 00:21:52.083
So knowing where we need
to put that subsidy,

00:21:52.083 --> 00:21:55.453
have that policy change
to drive the right kind of

00:21:55.453 --> 00:21:58.033
investment, and where the
market will take care of it,

00:21:58.033 --> 00:22:00.723
is based on really good data.

00:22:00.723 --> 00:22:03.313
And that data and funding
the ability to ensure

00:22:03.313 --> 00:22:06.763
that we've got a good mapping
of what the circumstances are

00:22:06.763 --> 00:22:09.343
in the United States
is another key element

00:22:09.343 --> 00:22:11.413
I think of what happens to inform

00:22:11.413 --> 00:22:13.146
really good policy moving forward.

00:22:14.694 --> 00:22:17.043
- And what do you think
the odds are it'll happen?

00:22:17.043 --> 00:22:19.686
You think people you're
talking to are aligned?

00:22:21.253 --> 00:22:24.913
- You would think, I think
there's good probability

00:22:24.913 --> 00:22:27.453
it can happen, although,
I'm the last person

00:22:27.453 --> 00:22:31.033
to prognosticate on something
coming out of Washington.

00:22:31.033 --> 00:22:34.483
It's oftentimes hard to
get the calculus of things.

00:22:34.483 --> 00:22:36.393
But if you think about it today,

00:22:36.393 --> 00:22:39.533
it's truly a bipartisan issue is,

00:22:39.533 --> 00:22:42.803
many people you've had as
guests here will talk about,

00:22:42.803 --> 00:22:45.293
everybody's in favor of education.

00:22:45.293 --> 00:22:48.673
I think everybody understands
that it's a key issue

00:22:48.673 --> 00:22:51.753
to equality, it's a key
issue to opportunity,

00:22:51.753 --> 00:22:54.748
shouldn't be a Democratic
or a Republican issue,

00:22:54.748 --> 00:22:58.703
it should just be, it's good
for our society as a whole.

00:22:58.703 --> 00:23:01.233
So you start from that point of view.

00:23:01.233 --> 00:23:03.489
Second thing I would
say is that the dynamics

00:23:03.489 --> 00:23:06.026
kind of line up really well.

00:23:07.043 --> 00:23:09.923
If you think about red states today,

00:23:09.923 --> 00:23:12.833
many of those tend to be rural in nature.

00:23:12.833 --> 00:23:15.253
They have an internet access problem.

00:23:15.253 --> 00:23:17.193
And if you think about urban dynamics,

00:23:17.193 --> 00:23:21.983
which tends to be more
Democrat stronghold,

00:23:21.983 --> 00:23:23.993
they have an internet access problem.

00:23:23.993 --> 00:23:27.463
So if the two sides can
get together and figure out

00:23:27.463 --> 00:23:30.493
what we do in urban and what
we do to deal with rural,

00:23:30.493 --> 00:23:33.303
then you would think you have
a natural coalition of folks

00:23:33.303 --> 00:23:35.363
that wanna solve that problem.

00:23:35.363 --> 00:23:39.073
Infrastructure has traditionally
been a bipartisan issue.

00:23:39.073 --> 00:23:42.593
So, if there is something
that it would seem like

00:23:42.593 --> 00:23:44.193
there might be an ability to

00:23:44.193 --> 00:23:46.653
not only get some good policy done,

00:23:46.653 --> 00:23:50.303
but figure out how we drive
spending in the right place

00:23:50.303 --> 00:23:54.633
to come out of the COVID
dynamic, to equalize education,

00:23:54.633 --> 00:23:58.253
to stimulate the economy,
to help telehealth.

00:23:58.253 --> 00:24:02.133
It feels like there's enough
good news there for everybody,

00:24:02.133 --> 00:24:04.936
that they should want to be
part of solving this problem.

00:24:05.853 --> 00:24:07.064
- Yeah, I couldn't agree more.

00:24:07.064 --> 00:24:10.223
And I do feel like the
energy behind this issue,

00:24:10.223 --> 00:24:11.433
I've never seen more energy.

00:24:11.433 --> 00:24:13.973
So fingers crossed.

00:24:13.973 --> 00:24:14.973
In the remaining time here,

00:24:14.973 --> 00:24:16.873
there's a bunch of
questions that have come in.

00:24:16.873 --> 00:24:21.473
And some of them are just on your journey,

00:24:21.473 --> 00:24:25.353
where Susanna Garcia Domingas
from YouTube is asking,

00:24:25.353 --> 00:24:28.083
hi, Mr. Stankey, please
tell us about your journey

00:24:28.083 --> 00:24:30.663
to become CEO, who are your heroes,

00:24:30.663 --> 00:24:32.933
and how do you reboot or
re-energize to keep your life

00:24:32.933 --> 00:24:35.333
in balance during these trying times?

00:24:35.333 --> 00:24:36.973
How did you get to where you are?

00:24:36.973 --> 00:24:39.176
And how do you handle it?

00:24:41.255 --> 00:24:43.073
- It's a really good question.

00:24:43.073 --> 00:24:46.923
And I'm always reticent to
answer this question because

00:24:48.588 --> 00:24:49.743
and I tell my children this,

00:24:49.743 --> 00:24:54.743
I grew up at a different time,
in a different United States,

00:24:54.923 --> 00:24:59.243
in a different way of learning,
than what is the norm today.

00:24:59.243 --> 00:25:02.363
And certainly, probably a
different social contract

00:25:02.363 --> 00:25:05.283
and different set of
companies that were relevant

00:25:05.283 --> 00:25:10.283
in the United States than what
a young aspiring professional

00:25:11.103 --> 00:25:12.953
might encounter today.

00:25:12.953 --> 00:25:17.353
And so, when somebody asks
me for advice, what I say is,

00:25:17.353 --> 00:25:18.663
don't do it the way I did it

00:25:18.663 --> 00:25:20.663
'cause that probably won't work anymore.

00:25:21.583 --> 00:25:24.673
What I did is I ended up
spending a lot of time

00:25:24.673 --> 00:25:27.473
and becoming quite deep in an industry,

00:25:27.473 --> 00:25:30.793
and building expertise
in a particular industry.

00:25:30.793 --> 00:25:35.013
I think I had some lessons
along the way when I was young

00:25:35.013 --> 00:25:38.663
that taught me at an early
age the value of hard work,

00:25:38.663 --> 00:25:42.533
and keeping your head down
and trying to do things well,

00:25:42.533 --> 00:25:44.263
and if that happened, then eventually

00:25:44.263 --> 00:25:46.313
you would be recognized for it.

00:25:46.313 --> 00:25:49.193
And I was fortunate enough in some cases,

00:25:49.193 --> 00:25:52.243
maybe even lucky enough,
to come along in my career

00:25:52.243 --> 00:25:55.643
with a couple of individuals
that took an interest

00:25:55.643 --> 00:25:58.093
in my career, that helped me out,

00:25:58.093 --> 00:26:01.193
that gave me opportunities,
that allowed me to stretch

00:26:01.193 --> 00:26:03.673
and maybe do some things that were beyond

00:26:03.673 --> 00:26:06.643
what my capabilities
on paper might've said.

00:26:06.643 --> 00:26:10.093
Lived up to those expectations,
and got succeeding

00:26:10.093 --> 00:26:14.093
in increasing levels of
responsibility as a result of that.

00:26:14.093 --> 00:26:16.353
Those lessons probably
aren't any different

00:26:16.353 --> 00:26:18.493
for today's environment.

00:26:18.493 --> 00:26:22.003
But what is different
is I went through the

00:26:22.003 --> 00:26:26.383
traditional degree structure,
get an undergraduate degree,

00:26:26.383 --> 00:26:30.563
have some pedigree in it, get
an opportunity to do a job,

00:26:30.563 --> 00:26:34.023
maybe go get an advanced degree,
have some pedigree in it,

00:26:34.023 --> 00:26:36.976
get an opportunity to do
something more significant.

00:26:37.933 --> 00:26:39.413
I think that's so passe.

00:26:39.413 --> 00:26:41.423
And it's not that I don't value education,

00:26:41.423 --> 00:26:42.413
it's quite the opposite.

00:26:42.413 --> 00:26:46.413
What I value is a lifelong
discipline to learning.

00:26:46.413 --> 00:26:49.963
And the reality is, I think
the successful generations

00:26:49.963 --> 00:26:52.563
moving forward, are
going to be individuals

00:26:52.563 --> 00:26:57.353
who are very disciplined and
thinking about in everyday

00:26:57.353 --> 00:27:01.003
as they wake up and every
month and every quarter

00:27:01.003 --> 00:27:03.783
and every year, what are their objectives

00:27:03.783 --> 00:27:05.883
for their own personal learning?

00:27:05.883 --> 00:27:09.563
How are they going to build
a curriculum on a variety

00:27:09.563 --> 00:27:13.343
of tools that are available
to them, to ensure that

00:27:13.343 --> 00:27:16.863
they're dedicating their
time and energy to do that?

00:27:16.863 --> 00:27:20.753
And they have to realize
that as this economy,

00:27:20.753 --> 00:27:22.943
it is our environment
has become more global,

00:27:22.943 --> 00:27:24.563
the internet has done that,

00:27:24.563 --> 00:27:27.513
you're competing with
people all over the globe,

00:27:27.513 --> 00:27:30.363
who everyday get up and have
access to some of the most

00:27:30.363 --> 00:27:34.513
remarkable sets of
information they've ever had.

00:27:34.513 --> 00:27:36.723
And if they're more motivated to get it,

00:27:36.723 --> 00:27:38.873
digest it, and learn it than you are,

00:27:38.873 --> 00:27:40.913
they're probably gonna be more competitive

00:27:40.913 --> 00:27:43.243
professionally than you are.

00:27:43.243 --> 00:27:44.913
And so you've gotta think about that

00:27:44.913 --> 00:27:47.373
as you're your own chief learning officer.

00:27:47.373 --> 00:27:49.403
You've gotta think about all the tools,

00:27:49.403 --> 00:27:53.113
capabilities are available
to you, like the Khan Academy

00:27:53.113 --> 00:27:56.923
and others, how you're
going to engage everyday

00:27:56.923 --> 00:27:57.893
to learn more.

00:27:57.893 --> 00:28:02.423
And if you do that, I think
you'll get more opportunity,

00:28:02.423 --> 00:28:05.323
you'll get increasing
levels of responsibility,

00:28:05.323 --> 00:28:07.693
and you'll keep your skills current,

00:28:07.693 --> 00:28:09.323
as things are moving and changing.

00:28:09.323 --> 00:28:14.163
And that, I think, is going
to be an incredibly important

00:28:14.163 --> 00:28:17.003
part of people's success moving forward.

00:28:17.003 --> 00:28:20.043
Balance, oh boy.

00:28:20.043 --> 00:28:22.913
I always talk about it internally is

00:28:22.913 --> 00:28:25.463
you have four dynamics in your life.

00:28:25.463 --> 00:28:27.033
It's like a box

00:28:27.033 --> 00:28:29.153
and you want that box
to be a perfect square.

00:28:29.153 --> 00:28:33.013
And the reality is, very few
of us are ever really good at

00:28:33.013 --> 00:28:34.763
making that box a perfect square.

00:28:34.763 --> 00:28:37.533
It's your personal relationships

00:28:37.533 --> 00:28:39.873
and those people that
are important to you,

00:28:39.873 --> 00:28:43.233
family, close friends,
that's one side of it.

00:28:43.233 --> 00:28:46.293
It's your personal
professional commitment,

00:28:46.293 --> 00:28:49.563
what you do at work and
what satisfaction you get

00:28:49.563 --> 00:28:52.453
out of that is one element of that box.

00:28:52.453 --> 00:28:56.423
The third element of it is why
do you get up in the morning,

00:28:56.423 --> 00:28:58.243
and what is your reason for being?

00:28:58.243 --> 00:29:00.683
It might be a set of religious beliefs,

00:29:00.683 --> 00:29:04.753
but you better be getting up
everyday and thinking about

00:29:04.753 --> 00:29:07.123
the fact that there's some greater entity

00:29:07.123 --> 00:29:10.623
you're serving, either your
coworkers, your fellow man,

00:29:10.623 --> 00:29:14.883
or somebody that's more important
than you that drives you.

00:29:14.883 --> 00:29:18.243
And the final and the
fourth area of that box

00:29:18.243 --> 00:29:21.183
is really your physical wellbeing.

00:29:21.183 --> 00:29:22.313
How healthy are you?

00:29:22.313 --> 00:29:25.393
How well you take care of
this vehicle that you have

00:29:25.393 --> 00:29:27.453
to get through life in?

00:29:27.453 --> 00:29:30.343
Everybody would rather
probably drive a Porsche

00:29:30.343 --> 00:29:33.563
than ride a scooter because
the Porsche performs better

00:29:33.563 --> 00:29:36.673
and your body needs to be
a Porsche, not a scooter.

00:29:36.673 --> 00:29:39.923
And so your job is to think
about your life everyday

00:29:39.923 --> 00:29:42.013
across those four dimensions.

00:29:42.013 --> 00:29:45.023
And when one side of that
square is out of balance,

00:29:45.023 --> 00:29:48.433
work to get it into balance
or try to keep it balanced.

00:29:48.433 --> 00:29:50.603
And I have to use a lot of discipline,

00:29:50.603 --> 00:29:54.673
it's self-evaluation
across those four dynamics.

00:29:54.673 --> 00:29:56.773
It's about setting limits.

00:29:56.773 --> 00:29:59.073
Limits to how my professional life

00:29:59.073 --> 00:30:01.173
creeps into my personal life.

00:30:01.173 --> 00:30:03.243
How much time I reserve in a given day

00:30:03.243 --> 00:30:05.793
to ensure that my body still functions.

00:30:05.793 --> 00:30:08.563
What am I doing for my own mental renewal?

00:30:08.563 --> 00:30:11.033
You have to be very disciplined
around thinking about

00:30:11.033 --> 00:30:13.413
those things and not being shy about

00:30:13.413 --> 00:30:17.083
building the coping mechanisms
and specifics to ensure that

00:30:17.083 --> 00:30:19.696
you're spending time in
all four of those areas.

00:30:21.233 --> 00:30:23.643
- Well, John, I could talk
to you for a few more hours

00:30:23.643 --> 00:30:26.063
about actually each of these topics,

00:30:26.063 --> 00:30:29.353
but you need to help
keep the nation's telecom

00:30:29.353 --> 00:30:30.333
infrastructure going,

00:30:30.333 --> 00:30:33.373
so that we can help the
nation keep learning.

00:30:33.373 --> 00:30:35.513
Thank you so much for this
incredible conversation,

00:30:35.513 --> 00:30:38.673
and thank you again for all of
your support over the years.

00:30:38.673 --> 00:30:41.293
- Sal, thank you for everything
you do at the Khan Academy.

00:30:41.293 --> 00:30:43.553
I'm more than happy to be affiliated,

00:30:43.553 --> 00:30:45.803
and proud I could spend some
time with you this afternoon.

00:30:45.803 --> 00:30:46.636
Thanks.

00:30:47.473 --> 00:30:48.773
- Thank you so much, John.

00:30:50.673 --> 00:30:52.073
Well everyone, thank you for joining.

00:30:52.073 --> 00:30:54.923
Hopefully you enjoyed that
conversation as much as I did.

00:30:55.763 --> 00:30:57.953
I find John to be an inspirational figure.

00:30:57.953 --> 00:31:00.506
And I have light in my eyes
now, the sun has moved.

00:31:02.033 --> 00:31:05.683
I really, hopefully we can
get him on in the future again

00:31:05.683 --> 00:31:07.833
to talk about some of the
life work balance stuff,

00:31:07.833 --> 00:31:08.666
which is I think,

00:31:08.666 --> 00:31:11.503
something that we're all trying
to struggle with these days.

00:31:11.503 --> 00:31:13.173
But anyway, thanks for joining.

00:31:13.173 --> 00:31:14.853
A lot of important issues there.

00:31:14.853 --> 00:31:18.043
Our next live stream will be on Thursday,

00:31:18.043 --> 00:31:20.003
where we have a surprise guest.

00:31:20.003 --> 00:31:21.923
We're going to actually announce

00:31:21.923 --> 00:31:25.503
and meet the winner of the
breakthrough junior challenge,

00:31:25.503 --> 00:31:29.153
which is this incredible
challenge where students

00:31:29.153 --> 00:31:31.753
from around the world submit these videos

00:31:31.753 --> 00:31:34.083
explaining new concepts
in math and science.

00:31:34.083 --> 00:31:37.393
And the winner gets hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:31:37.393 --> 00:31:40.243
worth of prizes and scholarships.

00:31:40.243 --> 00:31:44.223
So it'll be a pretty fun
conversation, I predict.

00:31:44.223 --> 00:31:45.223
I will see you then.

00:31:46.406 --> 00:31:48.489
